---
name: research-train
description: How to run and write up an investigation (train) in the recruit repo — from a question to a filled-out catalogue under analysis/ or a note under docs/sessions/. Load on "research X", "figure out why", "look into X and write it up", "look into X", when starting an investigation into data/pipeline, when writing up findings, when adding to an existing analysis. Accounts for the project's core constraint — the agent has no access to Postgres; all measurements go through ops/check scripts that the owner runs.
---

# Research train in recruit

A *train* is one investigation carried from question to a filled-in answer.
The output is not a document but a **catalogue** of artifacts aimed at
different readers. Getting the right set of artifact kinds matters more than
polishing any single file.

| artifact | answers | reader |
|---|---|---|
| `README.md` | "what is this and should I read it?" | someone browsing `analysis/` |
| `SUMMARY.md` | "what did we learn?" | someone who needs the model once |
| `INDEX.md` | "where is the fact I need?" | someone who already has the model |
| `organized-notes/NN-topic.md` | a verified detail, with citations | someone who will act on it |
| `messy-notes/topic.md` | raw findings, provenance | someone who suspects a fact got lost |

Plus `tools/` — scripts belonging only to this investigation, and `data/` —
their output.

## Where trains live

- **Full catalogue** — `analysis/<kebab-name>/`. Precedent:
  `analysis/domain_structure/` (phase scripts + JSON output side by side;
  there's no README there — that gap is a hole new trains should not repeat).
- **Small train** — a single note `docs/sessions/SESSION_YYYY_MM_DD_TOPIC.md`,
  in the style of `SESSION_SUMMARY_2026_08_25_OUTREACH_SELECTION.md`: it
  merges SUMMARY and INDEX in one file. Promote it to a full catalogue once
  the note starts having to choose what to leave out.
- **Closed train** → moves to `docs/archive/` in full (per `docs/README.md`'s
  rule: a dated, closed document moves to the archive — kept as history,
  but out of the way).

## Pipeline

**1. Fan-out — via subagents, in parallel.** Split the question into
non-overlapping topics, one collector per topic; run them in parallel
whenever subagents are available. Each writes a long raw note to
`messy-notes/<topic>.md`. Length is a virtue here; completeness matters more
than polish. A raw note is allowed to be wrong — that's what curation is
for — but it must be *specific*: file:line and command output, so a wrong
claim gets caught rather than merely doubted.

Constraints of this repo that fan-out must respect:

- the code lives on the owner's Mac and is only reachable via `device_bash`
  (`$HOME/mnt/recruit`); the cloud Bash cannot see the repo at all;
- **never query Postgres from the sandbox** (CLAUDE.md) — neither the agent
  nor any subagent; all data access goes through the "Measurement" step
  below;
- curation stays with one person: many collectors, but a single writer of
  organized-notes.

**2. Measurement — an async loop through the owner.** The agent cannot run
a query against the database itself. So every measurement is a read-only
script `ops/check/<name>.command`, which **the owner runs**, and the output
is pasted back into the chat and dropped into `messy-notes/` verbatim. The
loop: write the script → owner runs it → output goes into the note → next
cut. The script must print not just numbers but also **how to read them**
(a "what to look at" / "how to read this" block in the header or the
output) — a human without the agent's context is the one reading it.

**3. Cut before mechanism.** Reading the code and finding a plausible
mechanism is a hypothesis, not a diagnosis: the mechanism can be real and
still explain only 8% of the problem (that's how two hypotheses died in a
row on 28.08 — the crawler's mute path, then the template extractor). The
order is mandatory: first a cut script across several dimensions at once
(source, extractor, month, role…) — wherever the share deviates from the
overall rate, that's where the cause is; only check a mechanism afterward,
and only with **kill predictions**: "if the hypothesis is true, the data
must show X, otherwise it's false" — and print the prediction's failure
loudly rather than rationalize it away.

**4. Curate.** Read all the raw notes yourself and rewrite what survives
into `organized-notes/NN-topic.md`, numbered in reading order. Curation is
not a summary: contradictions get resolved by going back to the source,
unverified claims get dropped, and a finding two collectors reached
independently gets merged. **Every discrepancy you resolve gets named in
`INDEX.md` §Status** — that's the contract that makes messy-notes safe to
keep around: without it, the reader has no way to know which raw claims
were actually checked.

**5. SUMMARY.md — last.** This is a model, not a recap: the problem, the
root cause *and how it was established*, the cost, options in a ranked
table, a recommendation, and — the thing most often skipped — **the
decision that is actually in front of the owner**, phrased as a decision.
If the investigation closed that decision, say so and say why.

**6. INDEX.md — a routing table:** `| file | answers |`, one row per
organized note, phrased from the reader's point of view ("full contract for
Telegram rate limits", not "notes about limits"). Plus a "Question → where
to look" section with the actual questions people will show up with. Link
to neighboring trains.

## Rules that make notes worth keeping

- **Every number carries the date it was measured and the recount command.**
  House rule of the project (CLAUDE.md): "a number without a date is a lie;
  with a date, it's evidence. Recount with the command, don't trust the
  document." Form:
  `53.6% (measured 28.08, recount with zsh ops/check/record_density.command)`.
- **Every load-bearing claim carries a tag**: `verified` / `from memory` /
  `inferred`, inline. For claims about code, use the AGENT_RULES §11
  triad — **exists ≠ wired up ≠ deployed** — and the tag says which of
  the three states was actually checked. "From memory" is an instruction
  to go verify it, not something that can stay in organized-notes as is.
- **A cheap falsifier before writing down a claim.** If a grep, a single
  command, or one run settles the question, the note gets the result, not
  the reasoning it would have replaced (AGENT_RULES §2: runs find what
  reading can't; §1: before SQL, run `zsh ops/check/verify_sql.command`,
  keeping in mind it doesn't scan `.command` files — check their SQL
  against the models by hand).
- **Record negative results.** A dead hypothesis is the most expensive
  thing in a train to re-derive and the easiest to lose. Name it, name the
  cause of death, and name the measurement that killed it (example:
  `SESSION_2026_08_27_MONITORING_PANEL.md` §4e: two dead hypotheses named
  explicitly, so the next investigation doesn't re-chase them).
- **Enumerate, don't sample, inside a survey.** A note surveying a set —
  flags, columns, scripts, roles — lists *every* item with a verdict. A
  sample can't structurally prove nothing was missed. Unimportant items go
  into an appendix table, but not into nowhere.
- **Full-spec detail is the standard for organized-notes.** A verified note
  is detailed enough that the core ideas can be implemented from that one
  document alone — only small nuances are missing. Test: could the next
  agent pick up or continue the work without re-digging through the chat?
- **Status banners get a date, not a rewrite.** When reality has moved past
  a document, don't rewrite it into a lie — add a dated banner at the top
  (`⚠ Update DD.MM HH:MM`, as in NEXT_SESSION_QUEUE) naming which sections
  describe the past; leave the rest alone.
- **Scripts go into the train's `tools/` OR into `ops/check/`, deliberately.**
  A script placed in `ops/check/` enters the registry (needs a header
  banner `# name.command — description`, or
  `test_every_ops_script_has_a_header` fails) and, if classified
  `read_only`, becomes runnable from the "System" panel. A one-off script
  goes into the train's `tools/`, its output into `data/`, and the note
  cites both. Traps: a file created through the bridge needs `chmod +x`;
  inside a `.command` file it's zsh (`\b` in a Postgres regex means
  BACKSPACE, a glob with no match aborts the command, `read -k` fails
  under bash) — AGENT_RULES §9.

## Oddities — to the owner, as their own section

Anything non-obvious found along the way — logic that looks like a bug but
might be a deliberate decision — **is never silently normalized and never
silently fixed**. Every oddity gets recorded in `SUMMARY.md` under an
"Oddities" section: the observation (with file:line or a measurement) plus
a direct question to the owner, "is this intended or not?" The owner
decides; the verdict gets appended to the item. Examples of the genre:
`run_extract_vacancy.command` is byte-identical to
`run_extract_candidate.command` (matching md5 — vacancy extraction has
never actually been run); `check_invalid_fields.command` writes to the
database from a supposedly read-only diagnostics folder; the mask covering
699 tools is identical across every role. Each of these facts would have
been "fixed" incorrectly without asking the owner first.

## Formatting — house style

All artifacts are written in Russian (except identifiers and paths). Note
H1: `# <topic phrase with the outcome> — DD.MM.YYYY`. The preamble is a
blockquote: **Trigger** (what started the train, linking to the queue/
complaint), method and how it was checked ("verified by a run, not by
reading — AGENT_RULES §2"), **Rig** (script/harness, path, run time), and a
line about the numbers ("Numbers — measured DD.MM on a snapshot from
DD.MM…"). Numbers use thousands separators (`89 588`), deltas are written
`Δndcg −0.006`, dates in prose are `DD.MM` when the year is obvious.

## README.md — frontmatter

```yaml
---
kind: analysis
status: live | paused | finished | closed | superseded
updated: YYYY-MM-DD          # absolute dates only
start: SUMMARY.md            # the one file to open first
subject: subsystem · git-hash-of-measurement   # prod = code on disk; hash, not a branch
next: none | one sentence on what will resume the train
---
```

Then two paragraphs: why the train happened, how it ended, where to start
reading. There's no frontmatter validator in this project (unlike the
source workspace with its `status.py`) — discoverability instead relies on
`docs/README.md`: a new train gets registered there in the navigation in
the same session it's created.

## Worked example

The 28.08 train, "why 53.6% of open vacancies have no requirements"
(written up in `SESSION_2026_08_27_MONITORING_PANEL.md` §4a–4e): five
read-only scripts (`record_density` → `requirements_gap_provenance` →
`template_pass_audit` → `empty_requirements_cuts` → `extract_dryrun`), each
run by the owner; two hypotheses killed by predictions and named
explicitly; the cuts narrowed it down to two regressions; a run found the
root cause (a 12k-token prompt against the local model's context window),
and the fix was verified by that same script before the result was written
down. This is the reference case for the "script → owner → output → next
cut" loop.
